course: dictionary
lesson: 1958
title: Comparing with comparatives
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A comparative word helps you compare two things. Short adjectives usually add -er: big becomes bigger, tall becomes taller. Longer adjectives use more: beautiful becomes more beautiful.
explain: «The comparative compares two (bigger, more beautiful); the superlative names the top one (biggest, most beautiful); long adjectives use more and most.» So use the comparative for two things and the superlative for the top one of three or more.
explain: Ask yourself: am I comparing two things? If yes, you need the comparative. Try it in a sentence: «Ben is taller than Sam» uses the comparative, because it compares two boys.
fact: Comparative means the form of an adjective or adverb used to compare two things.
fact: «Bigger» and «more beautiful» are comparatives.
fact: «Comparatively» means judged by comparing with something else, as in «comparatively easy».
fact: Short adjectives usually add -er, and longer adjectives use more.
fact: Comparative can also be a noun: «a comparative», plural «comparatives».
exercise: «Ben is taller than Sam.» Which word is the comparative? | Ben | Sam | *taller | than
exercise: Which is the comparative of big? | most big | *bigger | big | biggest
exercise: Which sentence compares two things correctly? | The red bag is more heavy than the blue one, very. | The blue bag is the heaviest of the two. | The red bag is heavy than the blue one. | *The red bag is heavier than the blue one.
test: Which is the comparative of beautiful? | *more beautiful | beautifuller | beautifullest | beautiful
test: «Ana is the fastest runner in the class.» Is fastest a comparative or a superlative? | noun | adverb | comparative | *superlative
exam: Which sentence uses the comparative correctly? | *Tom is faster than Sam. | Tom is the fastest than Sam. | Tom is more faster than Sam. | Tom is fast than Sam.
